# Model of the human's war decisions: method

Written before the data from the war-log snapshot DLL arrive (`builds/war-log/README.md`), so
that each game can be analysed as soon as it comes in. It fixes the questions, the rules
compared, how they are judged, and what would refute each one. No figure is stated here: the
results go to their own document, through the registries the tools write.

## 1. Purpose

The model stands in for the human in the human's seat. It has two uses:
- **A fixed opponent.** AI changes are judged against it (`framework.md`, section 7).
- **A measure of the gap.** The same lines are written for an AI seat, so the human's
  decisions and the AI's can be set side by side in the same terms.

It is a small set of decision rules, each fitted on the human's decisions and judged on games
not used to fit it. It does not copy moves. The earlier war model (`HarnessHuman.cpp`) is
retired for the reasons in section 2: its thresholds were correlates tuned on pilot runs.

## 2. What the literature sets

- **Satisficing.** Simon, as given in *Heuristics* (Gigerenzer, Hertwig and Pachur, chapter 1):
  a person sets an aspiration level and takes the first option that meets it. The declaration
  rule takes this form: declare at the first turn start at which the chance of taking a city
  in reach meets an aspiration level `q`. That is one parameter.
- **Few parameters.** The bias–variance dilemma (*Heuristics*, chapter 1): with few decisions,
  a rule with one free parameter predicts better than one with many. Every rule here has at
  most two parameters, and enters the model only if it beats the base rate on held-out games.
- **The cue is computed, not estimated.** Lanchester's law of one-to-one combat (*Aircraft in
  Warfare*, chapter V): an assault is a sequence of duels. The chance of taking a city is the
  engine's own forecast (`AI_assaultForecast`), written into each `J` line.
- **Strategy classification.** Bröder (*Heuristics*, chapter 17), as in `tools/strategy.py`:
  the target choice is explained by the strategy under which the observed choices are most
  likely, against guessing.
- **The game is the unit.** Mandelbrot and Wallis (Noah, Joseph and operational hydrology):
  decisions within one game are one record, not independent draws. Every rule is fitted
  leaving one game out and scored on that game. Pooled tests are read with this caveat.
- **Errors compound off the human's path.** Ross, Gordon and Bagnell (a reduction of imitation
  learning to no-regret online learning): a policy fitted on an expert's states can make errors
  that grow with the square of the horizon, because it reaches states the expert never saw.
  Fidelity on the human's own states is therefore necessary but not enough (section 5).
- **Training and test.** Luke (*Essentials of Metaheuristics*, section 11): the games and seeds
  used to fit the model are never used to judge it. Outcomes need at least `30` seeded runs,
  compared by ranks.

## 3. Decision classes

The state of a decision is what the human had and saw as the turn began: the `I` header of the
turn start and the `L`, `J` and `O` lines after it. The decision is what the human did before
the next turn start. `tools/hmwar.py` builds the cases; each class has a label `y`.

| Class | Case | `y` | Cues | Rules compared | Refuted if |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Declare | A turn start with a team met, at peace, holding a city in reach (`J`) | War declared on any team (`W`) | Best capture chance within one and within three plots; own units near that city; already at war; open borders with that team; that team at war elsewhere | Satisficing on the capture chance (`q`); a hazard logistic in it; `models.py`'s trees and tallies on the binary cues; the base rate | No rule beats the base rate on held-out games |
| Target | A declaration | The team declared on | Per candidate team: best capture chance, own units near | Highest capture chance; most units near; satisficing (most units near among those reaching `q`); guessing | Guessing is as likely as every strategy |
| Assault | A turn start at war, with own units next to an enemy city | That city attacked (`C`) | Capture chance within one plot; city defence; defence fallen since the last turn (bombarded); own siege unit adjacent | Satisficing on the capture chance; the hazard; the trees; the base rate | As for Declare |
| Peace | A turn start at war with a team | Peace made (`Q`) | A city of that team in reach at an even chance; a city of it taken in this war | The trees on the binary cues; the base rate | As for Declare |

A declaration on a team with no city in reach is counted, not compared. Many of them would
refute "the human declares when the army is in place".

## 4. Fitting and judging

1. `hmwar.py` writes the cases per game, fits the satisficing level and the hazard leaving one
   game out, and classifies the target choices.
2. `models.py` scores the base rate, the single best cue, fast-and-frugal trees, tallying,
   logistic regression and a classification tree on the same cases, leaving one game out, with
   McNemar's test against the base rate.
3. A rule enters the model if it has more held-out hits than the base rate (McNemar). Where it
   gives chances, its held-out log score must also beat the base rate's. Otherwise the decision
   stays with the AI's own code, and the model says so.
4. The model is then frozen: no parameter changes after test results have been seen.

## 5. The model in its own states

The rules are built into the harness's human seat. With `WAR_LOG_SEAT` set to that seat, the
harness writes the same lines a human game does, so `hmwar.py` builds the same cases from the
model's runs. Compared on the same classes:
- **The fitted levels.** The `q` refitted on the model's own decisions must fall within the
  range the human's games give.
- **The states reached.** The share of the model's decision states whose cues lie outside the
  range of the human's states measures how far the model drifts from the human's path.
- **Outcomes.** Cities taken and lost by fixed turns, over at least `30` fresh seeds, against the
  AI in the same seat (`framework.md`, section 7).

Correcting drift needs the human's decisions in states the model reaches. *Open:* a harness
state cannot yet become a save the human can load (`sav2dat.py` converts in one direction only).
Until it can, the human's own autosaves from the turns before a war serve as decision probes.

## 6. What stays descriptive

These inform the rules' inputs but are not decision classes yet, and the tool does not compute
them yet (*open*):
- **Fights:** the odds of each attack, the order of attacks on one city in one turn (siege
  first, best odds first), and each attacker's experience and promotions (`C` joined to `L` by
  unit id).
- **Build-up:** the human's units by combat class, with their experience, by turn before each
  declaration (`L`, `U`, and the upgrade and promotion commands in `A`).
- **Staging:** where the units of the first attacks stood and where their moves were heading in
  the turns before the declaration (`L` positions and queued missions), and whether ships
  carried them (`transport`).

## 7. When a game arrives

1. `tools/extract.py <WarLog.log> <game.duckdb>`, with the save dump of its last save
   (`toolchain/analysis/dump.sh`) as `--state`.
2. `tools/hmwar.py <cases dir> <registry> <game.duckdb>:<human player> ...` over every game
   with snapshot lines.
3. `tools/models.py <class> <registry> <cases dir> <games>` for Declare, Assault and Peace.
4. The results document is written from those registries.

## 8. Assumptions and open questions

- **What the human sees.** The forecast is the engine's own calculation, and the human never
  sees it; the human sees the odds of each single attack. The forecast stands in for the
  human's own estimate. This is refuted if the odds of the best single attack predict the
  declarations better than the forecast does; those odds are not logged at the turn start yet.
- **Reach.** Cities further than three plots from the army have no `J` line, so a target still
  far away is out of reach at the declaration. The count of such declarations (section 3)
  shows whether three plots is too short.
- **Sample.** Each game is one record. More games matter more than longer ones, and decision
  probes add the most decisions per hour of play.
